<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Feodor I of Russia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1557">1557</a>–<a href="/years/1598">1598</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Fyodor I Ivanovich · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik-dynasty">Rurik dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Feodor I was tsar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> from 1584 to 1598, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan IV</a> and the last of the Rurikid line to rule Muscovy. Pious and withdrawn, he left government to his brother-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boris-godunov">Boris Godunov</a>, under whose regency the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> Patriarchate was established in 1589 and Russia recovered towns on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-finland">Gulf of Finland</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. His death without an heir brought Godunov to the throne.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/feodor-i-of-russia.jpg" alt="Feodor I of Russia" width="320" height="446" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Shakko · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fedor_ioannovich01_reconstruction.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Feodor was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> on 31 May 1557, the third son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan IV</a> by his first wife Anastasia Romanovna. His mother died when he was three, and he grew up in the shadow of a distant father with only one surviving older brother, Ivan Ivanovich. He was sickly and diffident, extremely pious, and fond of visiting churches and having the bells rung, which earned him the name Feodor the Bellringer. In 1575 he married Irina Godunova, sister of the minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boris-godunov">Boris Godunov</a>. Ivan Ivanovich died on 9 November 1581, leaving Feodor and the infant Dmitry as the only sons.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan IV</a> died in March 1584, and Feodor was crowned tsar and autocrat of all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in the Dormition Cathedral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> on 31 May 1584. Historians have debated whether an assembly, the Zemsky Sobor, elected him; the few contemporary Russian sources say only that he took the throne by his father's blessing. Feodor was the nominal ruler. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boris-godunov">Boris Godunov</a> acted as de facto regent, and by the end of the 1580s he dealt with foreign powers independently. Irina also took some part in affairs, and three charters to monasteries between 1587 and 1597 are in the names of both tsar and tsaritsa.</p><p>In May 1586 the Shuysky family petitioned in the name of the Zemsky Sobor for Feodor to divorce his childless wife. He refused. The Shuyskys were banished from the capital and Godunov persecuted them heavily the following year. In foreign affairs Feodor declared the country open to all foreigners instead of keeping exclusive trading rights for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and dismissed the English ambassador Sir Jerome Bowes. Elizabeth I sent Giles Fletcher the Elder to protest, without success.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Irina bore a daughter, Feodosiya, in 1592, after almost twelve years of marriage, but the child died in 1594 aged two. On 17 January 1598 Feodor died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>; he was buried in the Archangel Cathedral in the Kremlin. After forty days of mourning the Zemsky Sobor elected Godunov tsar. Feodor's death ended the central branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik-dynasty">Rurik dynasty</a>, and the end of the line was later followed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/time-of-troubles">Time of Troubles</a>. Contemporaries described his reign as a time of prosperity and stability, though much of the credit went to Godunov. Feodor was venerated soon after his death and is listed in the Great Synaxaristes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Church</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Feodor I of Russia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereslavl-zalessky">Pereslavl-Zalessky</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=56.74,38.86&amp;year=1583" aria-label="Pereslavl-Zalessky on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5389 237 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Feodor I of Russia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5839 507h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5839 507h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=56.74,38.86&amp;year=1583">What was here in 1583? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
